  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Thursday...\n\nEarly this morning, an upper level low over the Great Basin \npushes this direction, increasing moisture over the area. \nAdditionally, a Canadian trof slides in from the northwest, \nbringing colder air and additional upper level support for \nprecipitation. Then, an associated cold front moves through this \nafternoon and evening, bringing widespread breezy northwest winds \nof 30 to 45 mph, 40 to 55 mph over the foothills, and cooling \ntemperatures.\n\nRain and mountain snow will begin over the west this morning and \ngradually spread east through the day. The cooling temperatures \nmay allow for precipitation to change over to a rain/snow mix or \neven all snow over the higher foothills. With the breezy winds and\npotential for snow by late afternoon, a Winter Weather Advisory \nhas been issued for Judith Gap from 3 PM today through 6 AM\nSaturday. Snow accumulation will generally be limited to the\nmountains and higher foothills. Currently, 4 to 8 inches of snow \nare expected over the highest peaks of the mountains.\n\nSheridan co WY and far eastern Montana remain hot and dry through\nmuch of the day, with a chance (10-40%) of light precipitation \nnot arriving until this evening. Prior to the cold front, breezy \nsouth to southwest winds of 30 to 40 mph set up east of a Sheridan\nto Baker line by late morning. There is a low chance (20%) for \nmountain wave activity over the Bighorn foothills with the \nsouthwest winds, which would bring stronger gusts of 40 to 50 mph.\nThen, as the cold front moves through this evening, winds turn out\nof the northwest, with gusts of 40 to 50 mph into the overnight \nhours. With the hot, dry, and breezy conditions, a Red Flag \nWarning is in effect from noon through 9 PM today over portions of\nsoutheast Montana and northeast Wyoming into northwest South \nDakota.\n\nThis weather system exits early Saturday, allowing for seasonable\ntemperatures through the weekend. Late Sunday into Monday, another\nupper low clips southeast Montana, allowing for a chance (20-50%)\nof precipitation over the southeast. Then, a Pacific trough looks\nto move in Tuesday, bringing a more widespread chance (30-60%) for\nprecipitation through Wednesday and cooling temperatures into\nThursday. There is still uncertainty in the timing and intensity \nof this Pacific trough, but there is increasing confidence in \ncooler temperatures allowing at least a little snow at lower \nelevations. Archer",
